course: history
lesson: 37
title: Britain and Ireland: a short story
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Britain was settled wave after wave by Celtic Britons, Romans, Anglo-Saxons, Vikings and Normans, and in modern times by people from all over the world, so English is a mix of many languages, like a stew with something from everyone who came to dinner.
explain: In 1066 William of Normandy won the Battle of Hastings, and the Bayeux Tapestry tells the story in pictures like a giant embroidered comic strip.
explain: In 1215 barons forced King John to accept Magna Carta, saying even the king must obey the law, and over centuries Parliament, not the king, came to make the laws.
explain: The United Kingdom grew from England, Wales, Scotland and Ireland, and by 1900 Britain ruled a huge empire, which spread English but also ruled many people without their consent.
explain: Ireland has its own long story: monks who made the Book of Kells, the terrible Great Famine, independence in 1922 and peace in Northern Ireland in 1998.
fact: Britain was settled over time by Celtic Britons, Romans, Anglo-Saxons, Vikings and Normans, and in modern times by people from all over the world.
fact: In 1066 William of Normandy won the Battle of Hastings, where King Harold died, and became king of England; the Bayeux Tapestry tells the story in pictures.
fact: After 1066 French became the language of the rulers, and thousands of French words entered English.
fact: In 1215 English barons forced King John to accept Magna Carta, which said that even the king must obey the law.
fact: After a civil war in the 1640s and later struggles, Parliament, not the king, made the laws, and today Britain has a monarch who reigns and an elected government that rules.
fact: England and Wales were joined with Scotland in 1707 and with Ireland in 1801 to make the United Kingdom.
fact: The Union Jack combines the crosses of England, Scotland and Ireland; Wales is not shown on it.
fact: By 1900 Britain ruled about a quarter of the world's land and people, from India to Africa and Canada, and most colonies became independent in the 1900s.
fact: The British Empire spread the English language and British laws, but it also took land and wealth and ruled people without their consent.
fact: Irish monks made beautiful books such as the Book of Kells and kept learning alive across Europe, and Saint Patrick is Ireland's patron saint.
fact: From 1845 a potato disease destroyed Ireland's main food; about a million people died in the Great Famine and over a million emigrated, many to America.
fact: Most of Ireland became independent in 1922 while Northern Ireland stayed in the UK, and after years of conflict the Good Friday Agreement of 1998 brought peace.
fact: Queen Elizabeth II reigned for 70 years, from 1952 to 2022, longer than any other British monarch, and her son became King Charles III.
exercise: Which of these peoples settled in Britain long ago? | Anglo-Saxons | The Aztecs of Mexico | The Mongols of Asia | The Inca of Peru
exercise: Who won the Battle of Hastings in 1066? | William of Normandy | King Harold Godwinson of England | King John of Magna Carta | Alfred the Great of Wessex
exercise: What tells the story of 1066 in pictures? | The Bayeux Tapestry | The Book of Kells from Ireland | The Mona Lisa painting | The Magna Carta document
exercise: Why does English have so many French words? | French became the rulers' language after 1066 | The English invented French and gave it to France | French was taught in every English school in the year 800 | Shakespeare was French
exercise: What did Magna Carta say? | Even the king must obey the law | The king can do anything he likes, with no limits at all | Peasants must pay double tax | Only France may have a king
exercise: In which year was Magna Carta agreed? | 1215 | 1066 | 1707 | 1922
exercise: Who makes Britain's laws today? | Parliament, with an elected government | The king alone | The barons | The Pope
exercise: When was Scotland joined with England and Wales? | 1707 | 1066 | 1801 | 1922
exercise: Which crosses does the Union Jack combine? | England's, Scotland's and Ireland's | England's, France's, Spain's and Portugal's | Wales's red dragon only | None, it has fifty stars
exercise: How much of the world did Britain rule by 1900? | About a quarter | About half | All of it | About a hundredth
exercise: What was a bad side of the British Empire? | It ruled people without their consent and took land | It spread the English language | It had far too many ships in its navy for anyone to count them | Nothing, it had no bad side at all
exercise: What is the Book of Kells? | A beautiful book made by Irish monks | A book of Viking laws | A Shakespeare play | A history of the Normans
exercise: Who is Ireland's patron saint? | Saint Patrick | Saint George of England | Saint Andrew of Scotland | Saint David of Wales
exercise: What caused the Great Famine in Ireland? | A disease destroyed the potato crop | A long and bloody war with Napoleon's France | Too much rain for one day | A huge plague of hungry rats
exercise: When did most of Ireland become independent? | 1922 | 1066 | 1801 | 1998
exercise: What brought peace to Northern Ireland in 1998? | The Good Friday Agreement | The great charter Magna Carta | The Treaty of Waitangi of 1840 | The Battle of Hastings in 1066
exercise: How long did Queen Elizabeth II reign? | 70 years | 45 years | 7 years | 100 years
test: Welsh kid at school asks why Wales isn't on the Union Jack. What's the answer? | The flag combines England's, Scotland's and Ireland's crosses, and Wales is not shown | Wales is the blue part | Wales has its own flag hidden inside | Wales joined in 2000
test: Dave reckons English is pure, never borrowed a word. What do you tell him? | It is a mix of many languages, with thousands of French words | He's right, every single word is pure old Anglo-Saxon from long ago | English comes only from Latin | English was invented in 1900
test: A king in 1300 wants to throw a man in prison without a trial. What might the man point to? | Magna Carta, which says even the king must obey the law | The Bayeux Tapestry, which shows that William won in 1066 | The Good Friday Agreement | The Union Jack
test: Why do millions of Americans have Irish ancestors? | Over a million people left Ireland in the Great Famine, many for America | Ireland was part of America | The Irish invented America | Everyone in America is Irish
test: How can the king be the head of the country but not decide politics? | He reigns as a symbol, but the elected government rules | He secretly decides everything behind closed doors every week | Nobody leads Britain at all | Parliament was closed in 1066
test: The British Empire is shown on a map as only good. What is fairer? | It spread laws and English but also ruled people without their consent | It was only good and every person it ruled was glad to be part of it | It was only bad and did nothing else | It never existed
test: mate says the irish famine was just bad luck with the weather. what's closer? | A potato disease destroyed the main crop, and about a million died | It was a short snack shortage | It was caused by a volcano in Ireland | Nobody actually died
test: In 1066 an English farmer goes to bed with one king and wakes up later with another. Why? | William of Normandy won the Battle of Hastings | Harold gave the crown away to his cousin in Norway as a gift | The Vikings voted | The barons signed Magna Carta
test: Ireland went from long conflict to peace in 1998. What does that show? | Peace can be made even after long troubles | Conflicts can never really end, they only stop for a while | Peace only comes from winning a war | Nothing changed in 1998
test: Who is the British monarch after Elizabeth II? | King Charles III | Prince William, her grandson | King George VI, her father | Princess Anne, her daughter
exam: Identify the principle established by the charter known as Magna Carta in twelve hundred and fifteen. | That the sovereign is likewise subject to the law | That the sovereign is above all law and answerable to no court | That Parliament shall be abolished | That Ireland shall be independent
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the effect of the Norman Conquest upon the English language? | Thousands of words of French origin entered English | English was entirely replaced by Latin in all spoken and written use | English acquired no new vocabulary | English became the language of France
exam: Ascertain the principal cause of the Great Famine in Ireland commencing in eighteen hundred and forty-five. | A disease destroying the potato crop | An invasion by the Normans | A prolonged drought throughout Europe | A great earthquake in Dublin
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the reign of Queen Elizabeth II. | It endured for seventy years, the longest of any British monarch | It endured for forty-five years | It concluded in nineteen hundred and fifty-two | It was the briefest of any British monarch
